mode: "100644"
text: "# YouTube Shorts algorithm 2026 — the verified signal stack\n\n*Volatile facts. Re-verify quarterly against YouTube Creator guidance and Studio analytics.*\n\n## The headline: Shorts are decoupled from long-form\nYouTube **fully separated the Shorts and long-form recommendation systems in late 2025.** They\nare **two growth engines on one channel** with different rules. Posting Shorts no longer\nalgorithmically boosts your long-form, and a Short that flops won't drag long-form down. Treating\nthem as one strategy is what quietly stalls channels. (Long-form is the sibling skill\n**youtube-long-form**.)\n\n## The deciding metric: swipe-through, not CTR\nLong-form lives on impressions + thumbnail CTR. **Shorts have no thumbnail and no click.** The\nalgorithm watches **Viewed vs Swiped Away** above all: did the viewer stay, or swipe? A Short\n**earns distribution by not being skipped.** Retention is the whole game from frame one.\n\n## Explore-and-exploit + the brutal first hour\nEach Short is tested on a **small interest-based seed audience** (not your subscriber count — small\ncreators can go viral). If they stay and don't swipe, distribution expands; if they swipe, it\nstops. **The test window is ~30–60 minutes:** clear the threshold in the first hour or the Short\nis largely dead on arrival. Hook and posting time matter more than ever. (Shorts can resurface\nweeks later if they find a new audience.)\n\n## Retention targets (read these in Studio)\n- **Viewed vs Swiped Away:** aim **~75%+** viewed. Below 50% at 10–15s → the hook needs work.\n- **Average percentage viewed:** **~70%+** is the internal \"good\" bar. **Over 100% = replays**,\n  one of the strongest positive signals.\n- **Swipe-away rate:** healthy **10–30%**; **>40% is a red flag.** High swipe-away but strong\n  retention-on-stayers → a packaging/first-frame problem, not a content problem.\n\n## Length (up to 3 minutes, but bimodal)\nShorts can run to **3 minutes** (since Oct 2024), but data is bimodal: **<30s** tends to higher\ncompletion; **~50–60s** tends to higher total views. Design the idea first, then the **shortest\nlength that still feels complete.** A tight 20s watched twice beats a meandering 90s abandoned.\n\n## Novelty is now mandatory\nAn **Anti-Repetitive Content AI** suppresses recycled formats/hooks and content too similar to\nwhat's already trending or what you've posted. There's an **information-gain expectation**: wide\ndistribution needs a genuinely unique angle. **Volume without novelty is a negative** — which is\nexactly why mass-produced templated AI Shorts run into trouble.\n\n## Two under-used facts + decay\n- **Shorts rank in search independently** (dedicated Shorts filter, early 2026) and appear in\n  Google search. Use **keyword-rich titles/descriptions** — most creators ignore this (social-seo).\n- **\"The Flattening\" (late 2025):** Shorts older than ~28–30 days get deprioritized. You can't\n  coast on a back catalogue; consistent new production matters. Post ~3–7/week, not spam.\n\n## Counting + monetization (post March 31, 2025)\nA view now counts when a Short starts to play/replay (no minimum), inflating raw view counts\n~30%. Use **engaged views** as the stable quality metric — it drives YPP eligibility and Shorts\nrevenue sharing. Captions matter: most watch **without sound**, captions lift completion, and\nYouTube indexes caption text for search.\n"
